        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Changes in the numbers of the immune cell subpopulations have been documented by US and Russian scientists.  Alterations in the immune cell function have also been observed such as the reduced mitogen-induced activation of T-cells, altered cytokine production, reduced natural killer cell (NK) cytotoxicity, and reduced delayed-type hypersensitivity responses to recall antigens applied intradermally during space flight.   This demonstrates that alterations in cell-mediated immunity do occur in vitro and in vivo.  Therefore, these results support the notion that the immune system is functionally altered during space flight.  The objectives of this experiment include (1) to determine phenotypic alterations in circulating immune cell subpopulations which occur during space flight and compare them to those observed immediately after flight (2) to assess functional changes in the peripheral mononuclear cells resulting from space flight and (3) to determine the roles of specific cytokines and immunoregulatory factors in mediating space flight-induced immune suppression.
<P>
APPROACH:<br>
Blood samples were obtained from each of  the three crewmembers preflight (150 and 35 days before launch), in-flight (FD 11),  and postflight (R+0 and R+9).  The different types of white blood cells were identified by specific cell-surface markers.  Staining of the cells, during flight, was performed using the Whole Blood Staining Device (WBSD).  The blood samples were injected into a plastic-reaction tube and stained with monoclonal antibodies.  The sample tubes were stored and transported back to the ground for analysis. The percentages of specific mononuclear cell subpopulations were determined by flow cytometry.  The basic immunophenotyping panel for analysis of circulating cell populations included the following markers: CD45 (leukocytes), CD14 (monocytes), CD3 (T-cells), CD16/CD56 (Natural Killer (NK) cells), CD4 (T helper/inducer cells), CD8 (T supressor/cytotoxic cells), CD19 (B cells), membrane-bound interleukin IL1a, and HLA-DR (Human Leukocyte Antigen). 
<P>
Mononuclear cells were assayed for monocyte, T-cell, and natural killer (NK) cell functions.  Mononuclear cell function was determined by the presence of early activation cell-surface markers.  Cells were incubated with mitogens for 24 hours to induce the early activation cell-surface markers.  Screening the cells for the early activation markers was performed by flow cytometry.  Cytokines and other immunomodulatory molecules were measured following the mitogen-induced activation.  Cell culture supernatants were harvested, frozen-stored at -70 degrees Celsius for subsequent analysis of cytokine production.  The concentration of cytokines and growth factors such as Interleukin-1a (IL1a), IL-1b, IL-2, IL-6 and IL-10 in the collected medium samples was measured by the Enzyme Linked Immunosorbent Assay (ELISA).  The culture medium concentrations of Prostaglandin E2 (PGE2) was measured by Radioimmunoassay (RIA).  Transcriptional levels of the cytokines were assayed in the flight and ground control cells by extracting and testing for messenger RNA (mRNA).  Natural Killer (NK) cell functions were assayed by the chromium release assay.  This assay measures the cytotoxic activity of effector (NK) cells by measuring the amount of chromium radioactivity released from the killed/lysed target cells.
<P>
RESULTS:<br>
Circulating subpopulations of peripheral white cells were not greatly altered during flight as compared to the preflight baselines.  In contrast, the samples taken within 2-3 hours after landing had the relative granulocytosis and lymphopenia as observed after most space flights.  A relative decrease in T-cells, monocytes, B-cells and NK cells was also noted after flight.  These changes were not observed in the in-flight sample taken during the last flight day, suggesting the changes are an acute response to reentry and readaptation to unit gravity.
<P>
In vitro activation of lymphocytes isolated from crewmembers during flight or immediately after flight was not significantly different from the control periods as determined by the expression of early activation markers.  Expression of CD 69 protein or IL-2 receptor (CD 25) 24 hours after mitogen activation was not altered relative to preflight control samples.  All crewmembers exhibited good NK cell function on landing day, however variable decreases in cytotoxicity were noted 9 days after landing.  The reasons for this effect are currently unclear, though it is consistent with previous observations during long durations space flight on Mir. 
<END>
